{
  "requirement": "`ToolUsage` holds the cumulative usage for one tool.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct ToolUsage {\n    pub calls: u64,\n    pub result_bytes: u64,\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for ToolUsage;\nimpl Copy for ToolUsage;\nimpl Debug for ToolUsage;\nimpl Default for ToolUsage;\nimpl Serialize for ToolUsage;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.ToolUsage`.\n\n**What the requirement states.** The text states only the type's static contract:\n- `ToolUsage` is a `#[non_exhaustive]` struct with public fields `calls: u64` and `result_bytes: u64`.\n- It implements `Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug`, `Default` and `Serialize`.\n\nThe behavioral obligations (increments, byte counting, replay, and keying by name) belong to the refining requirements `ToolUsage.calls`, `ToolUsage.result_bytes`, `ToolUsage.replay` and `ToolUsage.by_name`. Those have their own tests, and this subject doesn't need to cover them.\n\n**How the marked test covers each obligation.** `public_exports_match_the_appendix` in `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs` is the only {test} site for the subject. It runs `cargo doc` and parses the generated rustdoc HTML. For every non-module export it calls `check_declaration`.\n\n`ToolUsage` is in the public API inventory in `md/requirements/sdk/public-api.md`, with a declaration link to this requirement. `documented_exports` therefore reaches it and checks it against the requirement's own code blocks.\n\n| Obligation | Where it is checked |\n|---|---|\n| Trait implementations (`Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug`, `Default`, `Serialize`) | `check_declaration` parses the `impl …` listing from the requirement and asserts set equality with `RustdocSignatures::trait_impls(page)`. A missing or extra trait impl fails. |\n| Struct declaration, field names and types | `check_declaration` compares `signature_parts` of the requirement's declaration with the rustdoc declaration, in both directions. It reports \"Only in requirements\" and \"Only in Rustdoc\" differences. This catches a wrong field type, a missing field and an extra public field. |\n| Field members | It asserts that the indexed members (`calls`, `result_bytes`) equal `rustdoc.member_names(page)`. |\n| `#[non_exhaustive]` | The check requires that a published struct page with public fields shows `#[non_exhaustive]`. The expected declaration from the requirement also contains it. |\n| Export presence | The test asserts that the specified and documented export sets are equal. |\n\n**Gaps considered.** The requirement doesn't say what `Default` returns or how `Serialize` formats the value, so I found no further obligations to test. Counter behavior is delegated to the refining requirements.\n\nI found no gap and no unreviewed obligation. No {fixme} is involved.",
  "references": "0d76678f3ab137bf77b6a76d0cd17c460f60426827a412971b8cd589c65e25da",
